# ============================================================
#  Keep secrets and machine-specific files OUT of git.
#  If you ever run `git init` in this folder, these are ignored.
# ============================================================

# --- Secrets / credentials (NEVER commit these) ---
service_account.json
config.json
# backups/variants of config.json (e.g. *.pre-zip-import.bak)
config.json*
*.pem
*.key
.env
# App backup archives -- these bundle a full config.json with LIVE credentials, so a
# zip of the app must NEVER be committed. (e.g. "new app for brands shopify to amazon.zip")
*.zip

# --- ANOTHER COMPANY'S BUSINESS DATA ---
# The Orbit inventory capture. It holds live figures, ASINs, unit costs and
# screenshots for real agency CLIENTS (flux-footwear and others) -- not this
# app's data and not ours to publish. ORBIT_INVENTORY_HANDOVER.md section 9
# asks for a decision on this rather than a default, and out is the reversible
# direction: the files are on disk and can be added deliberately at any time,
# whereas a commit of a client's cost data cannot be taken back out of history.
# (This repo already has live API keys in its old history awaiting rotation.)
orbit_inventory/
orbit_inventory_complete.md
# The same applies to every other capture taken off Orbit: the PPC harvest and
# the phone-width measurements are somebody else's live account -- ASINs, spend,
# search terms, stock levels -- read to work out how their screens behave. The
# CONCLUSIONS belong in this repo; their data does not.
orbit_ppc/
orbit_mobile/

# --- Local data caches / runtime state (machine-specific, per CLAUDE.md §2 — never commit) ---
# These are written at runtime (they live on the persistent disk on the server), so they
# change every time the app runs -- they are app STATE, not code / bug fixes.
miles_bundles_store.json
miles_bundles.json
miles_harvested.json
miles_items.json
miles_ui_prefs.json
model_number_counter.json
attribute_defaults.json
app_state.json
# two-way Amazon<->app sync caches (feature 2): last-known-Amazon snapshots + live
# per-account pull-capability results. Runtime state, machine-specific -- never commit.
sync_snapshots.json
sync_capability.json
# ASIN Monitor: tracked-ASIN list + (later) offer-snapshot history. Runtime state.
asin_monitor.json
asin_monitor_history.json
# User accounts: emails, permissions and PASSWORD HASHES. Never commit -- a hash
# is not a password, but it is still credential material and it names every
# person with access to the app.
users.json
# Durable live-catalogue snapshots. Runtime state, per-machine, can be large.
live_snapshots.json
# The SQLite database. This is the DATA -- every listing, price, cost and note.
# Never commit it: it is large, it is binary (so every change is an unreadable
# diff), and it holds commercial information that has no business in a git
# history. -wal and -shm are SQLite's write-ahead sidecar files.
altascraper.db
altascraper.db-wal
altascraper.db-shm
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# --- Session baselines (bug-check snapshots, per CLAUDE.md §3 — never commit) ---
*.baseline.py

# --- Runtime / machine-specific ---
.venv/
.app_port
.DS_Store

# --- Python caches ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# --- Generated output folders (runtime; these become persistent-disk mounts on the server) ---
media/
uploads/
inventory_out/
ppc_out/
brands/
miles_templates/
files/
autofix_logs/
filled_unified_template.xlsm

# --- Stray local artifacts, not part of the app ---
python-3.14.6-macos11.pkg
KITCHEN_COOKWARE_SET_FOOD_SPATULA_KITCHEN_KNIFE_HARDWARE__2_.xlsm

# Miles harvest/generate run logs (local run history)
miles_runs/

# Live run heartbeat -- rewritten constantly while a run is going, local only
run_status.json

# Local run logs (beta app, dev server). Per-machine, never useful in the repo.
*.log

# Word lock files -- created while a .docx is open, deleted when it closes.
~$*.docx

# run_tests.py's lock, held only while a suite is running. Per-machine.
.run_tests.lock

# The disk-persistence marker. Written beside config.json on every boot, and it
# describes ONE machine's filesystem -- committing it would carry the dev box's
# boot history to the server and make the "has this folder survived a deploy"
# answer a lie.
.disk_history.json

# Which (account, marketplace) pairs Amazon is currently refusing. Per-machine
# runtime state, written beside config.json -- committing it would carry this
# box's failures to the server and rest marketplaces that work fine there.
marketplace_health.json

# The one-time copy of everything the Google Sheets held, taken before the app
# was detached from them. Megabytes of real listing data for real businesses --
# it belongs beside config.json on the persistent disk, never in the repository.
sheets_archive_*.json

# The HMAC key that signs public image URLs handed to Amazon. Secret, and
# must persist on the server -- see domain/image_urls.py.
image_url_key

# Manually-entered cost prices, per account and SKU (domain/cogs_store.py writes
# it beside config.json). This is what every product actually COST -- the single
# most commercially sensitive number in the business, and it is per-machine
# runtime state besides. Never commit it.
cogs_overrides.json

# CSV exports the app writes when the owner clicks Download on a report --
# real sales figures, generated on demand, dated in the filename.
sales_day_*.csv
sales_report_*.csv
orders_*.csv
listings_backup_*.csv

# Timestamped .bak copies the app makes before rewriting a rules file
# (e.g. sourcing_viability_rules.json.bak-20260816050743).
*.bak-*

# Screenshots taken while comparing the app against Orbit. Debugging aids from
# one session on one screen -- large, binary, and stale the moment the UI moves.
orbit_shots/
Screenshot*.png

# Sample images generated while testing the image pipeline -- large PNGs, not source.
image_samples/

# Tracker readings and watch list -- runtime state, machine-specific, like
# asin_monitor.json. Written beside config.json via domain/jsonstore.py.
trackers.json
.jsonstore.*.tmp
notify.json
compliance_scans.json
